e SERs for years at this point. One of my favorite examples, uh, is the search term\\n91|mortgage calculator, right? Where you type mortgage calculator and then there'd be a bunch of results. You'd\\n92|click on them and they had mortgage calculators. Well, Google decided that they would put a mortgage\\n93|calculator on the SER.\", \"total_lines\": 92, \"file_size\": 9982, \"truncated\": false, \"is_binary\": false, \"is_image\": false}", "tool_call_id": "call_089cb453c05641e99f07b9ba", "tool_calls": null, "tool_name": "read_file", "effect_disposition": null, "timestamp": 1787627903.4489055, "token_count": null, "finish_reason": null, "reasoning": null, "reasoning_content": null, "reasoning_details": null, "codex_reasoning_items": null, "codex_message_items": null, "platform_message_id": null, "observed": 0, "active": 1, "compacted": 0}, {"id": 439, "session_id": "20260825_100001_62bb70", "role": "tool", "content": "{\"content\": \"1| fabulous for users, horrible for the businesses that had spent all the money to rank for mortgage calculator\\n2|because suddenly the traffic went away. So in the first couple of conversations with chat GPT3 and a half,\\n3|it's just like man, Google is going to be able to take this information, this technology, and keep people on\\n4|the SER forever. So we pivoted very hard away from non-localformational type searches and said we're going to\\n5|do local SEO, right? However advanced AI is, it's still a couple of years from being able to replace people's\\n6|water heaters. So when we started doing local SEO, we did it in the same way that we did non-local. So uh the\\n7|money page for local SEO we started treating to be your Google business profile. So then we thought, okay,\\n8|everything on the website is supporting content to establish more trust, more authority for the Google\\n9|business profile. So we started testing this concept where whatever your categories and services on your GBP,\\n10|we would build the website to exactly mirror the GBP categories and services. So now we call that the core 30\\n11|cuz it for most local businesses it ends up being around 30 pages. And we have found that works so well\\n12|because when your website matches the GBP exactly, uh it builds so much trust not just with Google but with\\n13|the AI systems as well. So I think that's the simplest way for uh and a local business owner can do a lot of\\n14|that themselves uh you know by just prompting AI to write the content uh and the local business owner knows\\n15|obviously enough about that business to be able to get the AI to write something unique and interesting about\\n16|each of those categories and services and structure the website to match the GBP. I mean for 80% of markets\\n17|that alone will significantly increase rank and the number of calls you're getting even if you don't do\\n18|anything else. So I think that's like the lowhanging fruit and when somebody comes to me and says hey will you\\n19|do SEO for us? I mean that's like step one build out the core 30 and you know month one results almost always\\n20|just look fabulous because it's such a simple thing and you know the machines don't know how to interpret\\n21|messy signals the way that humans do. So we're just trying to create the same signal that makes sense match\\n22|your website to your GBP. That was a really long answer to that question. >> That's awesome. Could you give uh\\n23|could you give like an example of what that would look like? Right. So, uh I I do Yeah. So, your homepage is\\n24|going to be based on the GBP primary category, right? So, so say plumbers. When we started running ads for\\n25|local businesses, we ran ads for plumbers. So, we've had a lot of plumbing clients. So, my example always goes\\n26|to plumbers. So, say say plumber. So, for a plumber, your primary category is clearly going to be plumber. And\\n27|then your GBP landing page, which is usually your homepage. You're going to base that around like plumber city\\n28|name, right? Like plumber Houston. And then on your homepage, you're going to talk about the secondary\\n29|categories. And then a couple of what we call like core services, services that you really, really care about.\\n30|So for a plumber, your secondary categories might be drainage service, bathroom remodeling, and then the core\\n31|services that you really care about. Most every plumber I've ever talked to, the services that they want more\\n32|calls for are water heater replacement and main drain line replacement. So, we'll do an H2 for each of the\\n33|secondary categories and then an H2 for the core services and then a paragraph, you know, maybe 50 70 words\\n34|underneath that H2. And then we link to a page where that page is about bathroom remodeling or that page is\\n35|about water heater replacement. So that's going to be the H1, bathroom remodeling Houston. And under there,\\n36|we're going to talk about all the services that live under that secondary category on your GBP. Right? You're\\n37|when you set up your GBP services, you set up your categories and then you set up services under each\\n38|category. So the services under bathroom remodeler we talk about on that page and then each one of those links\\n39|to a page about that specific service. So maybe you know you have faucet replacement. So that's a service\\n40|under bathroom remodeler. You'll create a service page for faucet replacement Houston and then the bathroom\\n41|remodeler page links down to that service page. So that creates this exact same hierarchy structure that your\\n42|GBP has. >> What do you do with businesses that have multiple locations or multiple cities? >> Yeah. So for\\n43|that uh there are that is one of the two reasons why your GBP landing page might not be your homepage. So, uh,\\n44|essentially every location should have its own GBP landing page. Uh, but if you're taking on a new client or\\n45|you have an existing business and you set it up so that all of your GBPs point to your homepage, we don't move\\n46|them to an internal page until we run the rank map, right? And I'm sure you know this, Edward, but if you if\\n47|your GBP landing page is your homepage and then you move it to an internal page, your rank is going to\\n48|plummet. So, we never do that if they're ranking, right? So, we always look at the rank map and if their rank\\n49|is horrible, uh, or they're relatively new, then every single GBP gets its own GBP landing page. It's\\n50|basically just a copy of the homepage. Um, and we build up the core 30 for every single location. So, if you\\n51|have five locations, that becomes the core 150. But it's important to keep in mind that I love multilocation\\n52|businesses because as you're building all of this out, the domain is getting so much topical relevance and\\n53|authority that you'll hit a point when you're adding, you know, location number four, location number five,\\n54|depends on the competitiveness, etc., etc. But eventually you'll hit a point where you'll create a GBP landing\\n55|page. You'll point the GBP to it and then you'll be ranking reasonably well just because the domain has so\\n56|much authority and relevance for plumbing. You don't need the core 30 anymore for this new location. And then\\n57|we just go straight into producing geographical relevance content. >> Okay. What's the biggest when it comes\\n58|to Google business profile? What what what's the biggest mistakes that you see businesses making? >> Yeah. So,\\n59|uh, categories and services, right? Um, the most common businesses we look at, they'll have one category,\\n60|maybe two. Google allows up to 10. There's this myth floating around that if you add multiple secondary\\n61|categories, you're diluting the ranking power for your primary category. We've tested this over and over and\\n62|over again. We've never seen we've only seen positive results from adding more secondary categories. Uh and\\n63|then the other mistake is your services, right? Uh it and the mistakes we see there are everything from not\\n64|enough services to keyword stuffing, right? So we know that Google works based on entities. It doesn't use\\n65|keywords anymore. So the days where if you had a personal injury lawyer, you needed to do car accident lawyer,\\n66|car accident attorney, uh car wreck attorney, car wreck lawyer, right? So now we have four services that\\n67|describe the exact same thing. Google knows those are the same thing and keyword stuffing your services. I\\n68|don't know that it ever worked, but it certainly doesn't work today. So, that's where we spend most of our\\n69|effort for a new client is we're going to come in there, take a look at their categories and services and make\\n70|sure those are correct and optimized before we start building out the core 30. And it's not hard, right? Like,\\n71|but it's just something that most businesses don't do. Um, and then the other aspect is just making sure every\\n72|single box is filled out. The most common one that we don't see filled out is uh holiday hours because Google\\n73|doesn't let you put in holiday hours forever. You can only put in the holiday hours for like the next two or\\n74|three holidays. So, you need to log into GBP every few months and update your holiday hours for whatever\\n75|holidays are coming up. So, I mean, those are the the biggest mistakes that we see, but by far it's get your\\n76|categories and services correct. Yeah. >> How are you structuring the URLs when you're when you're Yeah. when\\n77|you have these like individual pages, when you have multiple locations, what does a website look like in terms\\n78|of URL structure and and and internal linking? Yeah, internal linking and hub pages and all of this stuff.\\n79|Yeah. So, the URL structure like I care very little about it. So, whatever my developer finds easiest to work\\n80|with to keep track of all the pages and everything, that's fine, right? Cuz Google doesn't build its image of\\n81|your website based on your URL structure. It builds what your website looks like based on your internal\\n82|linking structure. So I care a lot about internal linking and very little at all about URL structure. >> Do\\n83|you think that that Google looks at URL slugs or parent or parent folders? >> I don't think Google pays\\n84|attention to parent folders. Uh we will almost always make sure the slug is the service entity that we're\\n85|writing for. And that's just because it's pretty common that a lot of your links coming in will be the naked\\n86|URL. So having the keyword in the slug means you can put your keyword in the anchor text without being shady\\n87|or spammy. >> So So okay, so you would do So you would do like forward slash um Oh yeah, give me give me a\\n88|service. Give me a service. >> Sure. So it would be like, you know, domain.com heaterreplacement. >> Okay. So\\n89|you would do you would do domain.com/h houston/waterreplacement Houston. >> No. Well, if if if if you have\\n90|subfolders, then we would just do water heater replacement in that slug. >> Okay. >> Yeah. But normally we\\n91|don't do subfolders >> unless the developer or client really >> So, so if you have multiple locations, then\\n92|you're not you're not separating the URLs by location.\", \"total_lines\": 91, \"file_size\": 9907, \"truncated\": false, \"is_binary\": false, \"is_image\": false}", "tool_call_id": "call_dd4bc2b9e1a9443496fb440e", "tool_calls": null, "tool_name": "read_file", "effect_disposition": null, "timestamp": 1787627903.4621358, "token_count": null, "finish_reason": null, "reasoning": null, "reasoning_content": null, "reasoning_details": null, "codex_reasoning_items": null, "codex_message_items": null, "platform_message_id": null, "observed": 0, "active": 1, "compacted": 0}, {"id": 440, "session_id": "20260825_100001_62bb70", "role": "assistant", "content": "", "tool_call_id": null, "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_2023d82e6a334406aca1efdc", "call_id": "call_2023d82e6a334406aca1efdc", "response_item_id": "fc_2023d82e6a334406aca1efdc", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "read_file", "arguments": "{\"path\": \"/tmp/wc_03.txt\"}"}}, {"id": "call_a553a4413d3247eca6205d14", "call_id": "call_a553a4413d3247eca6205d14", "response_item_id": "fc_a553a4413d3247eca6205d14", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "read_file", "arguments": "{\"path\": \"/tmp/wc_04.txt\"}"}}], "tool_name": null, "effect_disposition": null, "timestamp": 1787627908.5047257, "token_count": null, "finish_reason": "tool_calls", "reasoning": "Alright, this is an interesting podcast. Caleb Ulku on Edward's podcast (probably Edward Sturm?). The content is about local SEO: the \"Core 30\" — a site structure that mirrors the GBP categories/services. Let me keep reading.", "reasoning_content": "Alright, this is an interesting podcast. Caleb Ulku on Edward's podcast (probably Edward Sturm?). The content is about local SEO: the \"Core 30\" — a site structure that mirrors the GBP categories/services. Let me keep reading.", "reasoning_details": null, "codex_reasoning_items": null, "codex_message_items": null, "platform_message_id": null, "observed": 0, "active": 1, "compacted": 0}, {"id": 441, "session_id": "20260825_100001_62bb70", "role": "tool", "content": "{\"content\": \"1| So, does that mean that you don't usually do do you have do you still have a hub page for the location? Do\\n2|you have forward/ houston? >> We might have like the slashorage serving as the GBP landing page for the\\n3|Anchorage location, but in terms of like all those subfolders and everything, I mean like I'm not going to go\\n4|create that. If it already exists, I'm not going to undo it either. I don't really care very much either way.\\n5|And that's because the most important you don't care because so so what are the key the key elements to\\n6|putting the keyword in for you? >> So the keyword ideally it should be in the URL slug. Uh but that's the\\n7|least important of the really important places to put it. The really important places to put it are the title\\n8|tag, the H1, and then the first paragraph. We also want to mention it very early on in the first paragraph.\\n9|Uh, a very common mistake I see among so many local business websites is their first paragraph of text is like\\n10|a history lesson about their business, right? And I I I make the joke, right? No one is typing plumber Houston\\n11|into Google because they're interested in reading history lessons about the local plumbers, right? So that\\n12|first paragraph, such a missed opportunity if you're not talking to the person who just typed plumber Houston\\n13|into Google and you know why you're the plumber that they should call, right? Like nobody cares about what\\n14|year you were founded and you know why your grandfather went into plumbing and why he immigrated from the old\\n15|world. Like nobody cares about that but that's where people start talking about local business websites. But\\n16|yeah, so title tag most important H1 and then in the first paragraph we'll start talking about it as we talk\\n17|to the searcher who landed on the page also important right because as you know there's an interview with\\n18|Sundar Pachai the CEO of Alphabet that came out I guess probably about a month or so ago now and one of the\\n19|things he talked about is that he sees Google evolving to become a home for agentic search. So I think what\\n20|that means you go to google.com and instead of a search box there's more like a prompt box and you type in\\n21|what you're looking there and then Google sends a bunch of agents out to find the information and bring it\\n22|back to you. So we're that is reaching a point where we're no longer writing websites for people. We're\\n23|writing websites for the AI agents to summarize those websites for actual people. So again, very important\\n24|when you're writing website content to make sure you're writing it for someone who is searching for what\\n25|you're looking for, whether that be the agents or actual people. >> Has that changed the way that you do SEO?\\n26|>> Not significantly. Um because the, you know, the right way to do SEO has always been to structure the\\n27|website in such a way that Google can understand it. And we always wrote for the algorithm. And then, okay,\\n28|let's also make sure we're writing for conversion for the people because, you know, if you have the best\\n29|website ever that's directed to people and you're ignoring the algorithm, it doesn't matter because no one\\n30|will ever see it, right? Uh assuming you're not running ads or something like that. Um so that like make sure\\n31|it's structured properly. Make sure it's written for the algorithm that stays. Um, and you know whether a\\n32|person is reading it or an AI is reading it, it doesn't really matter as long as you have the information on\\n33|there. So the biggest difference, right, is like a messy website or messy structure, people have the patience\\n34|to figure that out, whereas an AI doesn't. And on the flip side of it, the AI can read your entire website and\\n35|pick out the most important parts of it and give it back to a user. Whereas most users will spend, you know,\\n36|20 seconds on your site before they decide whether to call you or not. So that's where we get into like\\n37|attribute match matching uh which is something that we weren't super focused on before AIS, but now we know,\\n38|right? So if somebody searches for like lead main drain line replacement, the AI, whether it's chatt or claude\\n39|or Google's own Gemini, the AI is going to want to see that you're talking about lead main drain line\\n40|replacements. Well, if you don't talk about that, if you just talk about main drain line replacements without\\n41|using the word lead, you might not get recommended. Um, so that's uh that attribute matching is something\\n42|that's relatively new. Uh, when we're looking at content, building out a website for for local businesses. >>\\n43|Yeah, I always like I like to look at other pages that are that are ranking for keywords that I'm targeting\\n44|and I see the language that they use. Like basically like for me I'm a such a good SEO and I think you're you\\n45|I'm sure you're the same where you can go into any niche and within like a week or two understand the niche\\n46|well enough that you can actually write pretty expert content in it by just by taking notes of the the types\\n47|of language that appears throughout pages that are ranking for your target keywords. And then you make a list\\n48|of these. You you have your target keyword. You know where the target keyword goes. you have a hierarchy of\\n49|where the keywords are going, what's most important, what's least important, and then you can build your pages\\n50|like that. What do you think about um about AI images both on uh the website and in a Google business profile?\\n51|>> Yeah. So, the what you just talked about, we call that uh we've called that the open book test, right? So,\\n52|if you want to rank for whatever service entity you want to rank for, the best way to get started is to type\\n53|that into Google or type that into chat GPT and it will tell you, hey, here are my favorite pages about that\\n54|topic in the world. Uh, and then look at what they did and just do a little bit better, right? Like add a\\n55|little bit more information that they don't have. Uh, and there are variety of ways that we uh focus on to do\\n56|that, but yeah, exactly. the the point is to write local SEO content as the local SEO business or not as the\\n57|local business owner expert. >> Actually, another great question is is are you making uh do do you try to make\\n58|all the pages unique to the locations? Are you giving extra information based on location or like how do you\\n59|think about how do you think about it uh about doing that versus something like doorway pages? >> Yeah. So\\n60|every page we write for local SEO is hyperfocused on that location, right? So even, you know, I talked about\\n61|lead main drain line versus main drain line. So if we had a plumber client in the suburbs of Boston, talking\\n62|about lead main drain line replacements would be very important because that's a common issue in the suburbs\\n63|of Boston. But for a client in Southern California, we would never talk about that because they don't have\\n64|lead main drain lines in California. the hoses are a lot newer and that you know they use plastic main drain\\n65|lines. Um so those you know local differences are so important for local SEO. Um and we we actually saw an\\n66|issue I'm sure you remember is is like in early May where Google started mass de-indexing AI slop which you\\n67|know fabulous news. So when we build local content uh we we build it for there's four different types of local\\n68|content that we write. Uh so we write category content, we write service page content, we write topical\\n69|relevance content and then we write geographical relevance. So of those four types when Google started mass\\n70|de-indexing three of them no problem. But the geo content we started to see some de-indexing. So when I say\\n71|geo content, what we'll do is we'll run a local rank map for the target service entity. And everywhere that\\n72|you're not ranked in the top three, uh especially if you're ranked like four, five or six, we're going to\\n73|write hyper local content about landmarks in that specific area, right? So you know, again, using plumber\\n74|Houston as an example, we'll write content, you know, plumber in River Oaks, Houston, plumber near Montro,\\n75|Houston. uh plumber near uh 610 West and I 10 Houston. So, we're finding these hyper local landmarks like\\n76|lakes, zoos, city halls, colleges, whatever. Uh and we'll write content about a plumber in that region. Well,\\n77|for a very competitive space like Houston, we might end up with a couple dozen of those. And it's challenging\\n78|to make plumber Montros be a significantly different article than plumber River Oaks cuz they're so close to\\n79|each other. But if you know Houston, uh, Montros, uh, is a very different neighborhood than River Oaks, right?\\n80|River Oaks is very fancy estate sized homes. So you could write about the differences for being a plumber in\\n81|River Oaks because they are different than Montros. But how do you do this at scale, right? So what we started\\n82|doing is just an insane amount of research. So uh we would pull like US Census Bureau data, we'd pull Reddit\\n83|data, we'd pull all the data and information that we could find. We started hooking into the CRM so that we\\n84|could pull conversations and leverage all of this data to write content about, hey, why is being a plumber in\\n85|River Oaks different than being a plumber in Montro? Uh, and we did that, went back through the de-indexed\\n86|content and added all of this additional research and we got a lot of it to be reindexed because now it\\n87|actually isformational additive, right? We're not we're not writing the average of the content that's already\\n88|exists with redundant information. Now we're adding new information, right? River Oaks uh plumber is, you\\n89|know, you have to worry about walking 200 feet back to your truck because the front lawn is so big. Um, and\\n90|yeah, Montros is, you know, you might have a fiveplex or something that's two stories tall in Montros. Uh, so\\n91|yeah. >> Yeah, that's awesome. That is awesome. And and so how about AI images? Cuz you see this, you see this\\n92|from all all sorts of businesses trying to put in AI images. Um, but you also see in local. So what do you\\n93|think about Yeah.\", \"total_lines\": 92, \"file_size\": 9972, \"truncated\": false, \"is_binary\": false, \"is_image\": false}", "tool_call_id": "call_2023d82e6a334406aca1efdc", "tool_calls": null, "tool_name": "read_file", "effect_disposition": null, "timestamp": 1787627908.5458