o big and we need to rerun a a smaller rank map. Right? Uh our rule of thumb is we can do at\\n16|least as well as an existing competitor but we're never going to tell a prospective client that we can do\\n17|better than has ever been demonstrated in their area. Right? So, we're not going to run this giant rank map\\n18|where the top player is 30% top three and show the competitor and say we can dominate this space. Uh, and\\n19|depending on the competitiveness, the how large the city is, the rank map might be huge and it might be really\\n20|small. Uh, like we'll run rank maps everything from, you know, 5 600 square miles all the way down to like 30\\n21|square miles depending on how competitive the space is. always looking for the top players to be in that 60 70\\n22|to 90% top three. And obviously if the top players are 100% then the rank map is too small. We need to rerun\\n23|it larger because the ranking outside the the rank map. So uh we need to zoom out and and run it again. >>\\n24|Okay. So that's the first step to make sure that the rank map is the right size. >> What software are you\\n25|using? >> Then uh usually we use not usually we use this software called lead snap uh to run the rank maps. Uh\\n26|there's a bunch of other rankmap tools, but we started using lead snap because they have the a really great\\n27|API connection with citations. A lot of other tools do now also, but at the time they were the only one that\\n28|did. So that's why we went with them. >> Okay. So yeah, so next part. >> So next part. So uh then we're\\n29|looking to try to cross we call it the topical relevance threshold. So the topical relevance is convincing\\n30|Google that your business entity actually does that service entity. We're not trying to establish geographical\\n31|relevance just yet. We're just trying to say yes Google, we are a plumber in Houston. That's it. So we'll um\\n32|we we we say that when our clients top 3% is roughly half of the market leaders, we've established that trust\\n33|with Google, right? So you know top players in the 80% range. So once our client is 40% top three cool topical\\n34|relevance threshold met and we build topical relevance. The first step for topical relevance is of course the\\n35|core 30. And if we're in the competitive enough space where the core 30 is not enough. We've built out the\\n36|full core 30 and we still haven't hit that topical relevance. Then we start building supporting content. But\\n37|we build supporting content that supports their service entity. Right? So, well, it it would be something more\\n38|like what what to think about when you're hiring a plumber in Houston, not top five ways to prepare your water\\n39|heater for summer, right? So, it's always focused on that service entity and providing relevant content for\\n40|the service entity. And keep in mind, right, we might have four or five different rank maps depending on the\\n41|target services. So, for a plumber, we're always going to have three. plumber, uh, water heater replacement,\\n42|and main drain line replacement. So, we're doing this process three times for each of those three main\\n43|services we care about. We're not going to run rank maps for like unclogging a toilet because most plumbers\\n44|don't want that call. So, so we won't track it. And most of the things like that, if you rank for plumber,\\n45|you're going to rank for a lot of the stuff underneath it anyway. But once we hit that topical relevance\\n46|threshold, then we start building geographical relevance. And that's where, you know, let me find the four,\\n47|fives, and sixes, uh, where they're close to the top three, and then I'm going to build hyper hyper local\\n48|content about a local landmark around that dot. Uh, because and obviously it's important to use Google Maps\\n49|for that because you want to make sure Google recognizes that landmark. And uh, now we do it automatically\\n50|with like a Google Places API call. Uh, for the same theory, we want to make sure Google recognizes the\\n51|landmark. And if we're calling it with the Google Places API, then we know Google recognizes it because Google\\n52|gave us that landmark name. Um, and then we'll just write about yeah, replacing main drain lines around that\\n53|lake in Medford, Massachusetts. >> How are you doing internal linking with these posts? So, uh, it depends on\\n54|the competitiveness. For larger, like more competitive spaces where we might have dozens of supporting\\n55|content, we're going to create like a supporting content hub. Uh, and then we'll link to that hub either from\\n56|the GBP landing page, maybe the footer, and then that hub will link out to all of the supporting content\\n57|pages. and the supporting content links back to the service page we're trying to support, whether it be the\\n58|water heater replacement service page or the plumber primary category page. Uh the reason for that is, you\\n59|know, on the GBP landing page, if if we have a couple dozen supporting content pages, we don't want, you know,\\n60|25 links from the GBP landing page. So that's why we put that hub in between. >> Talk about AI. How has AI\\n61|changed your SEO strategy over the last 12 months? So, so far it hasn't done a lot to actually change our\\n62|strategy. Uh, it has significantly reduced costs. Uh, like our costs to produce an article and when I say\\n63|produce an article, like it's obviously to write all the content, it's to place it on the website, do the\\n64|internal linking, source an external link, it's do all of that stuff. Uh, our cost to do that is like roughly\\n65|80% lower than it was in mid 2022. Um, and AI has allowed us to write higher quality content, right? 2022, we\\n66|couldn't legitimately spend a couple of hours doing research for every single article we wrote. But now, we\\n67|can do the equivalent of hours of research with the right data calls and just have AI assemble everything and\\n68|pull all the research and assemble an outline and then start writing. And we get a level of quality that would\\n69|have taken hours. uh in 2022. Um so so far uh that's been the biggest impact is that we can write higher\\n70|quality content at scale than what we could in 2022 at a lower cost. But we are preparing for this world where\\n71|AI agents are primarily the ones reading everything. So I'm in the process of working through adding a fifth\\n72|content type for local SEO. Uh I talked, you know, right now we have four and we've had four for 10 years. Um\\n73|we're looking at uh building out this fifth type which we're calling trust. And the reason I feel like it's\\n74|different now is for like a plumber, the average user would spend 30 seconds on your page. So you didn't\\n75|really need a bunch of content about why that user should trust you because they weren't reading it. They\\n76|weren't looking at it. But now we can make the assumption that an AI agent is going to read everything on your\\n77|page. So having that trust content is suddenly quite valuable. So uh that's what we're working on. So it's\\n78|going to be like pricing information, uh specific stories about things that have gone wrong in the past. We\\n79|find that stories about things that have gone wrong are really really good at building trust. A lot of\\n80|businesses try to hide that. Um a lot of information, you know, why service might not be >> talk about that a\\n81|little bit. Oh yeah, that too. Yeah, these things. People wouldn't expect that. People wouldn't expect that.\\n82|So So why this might not be for you, things that have gone wrong. So So you're saying include that? Yeah,\\n83|100%. Because right the So we know of course that when somebody opens up Google and searches for something,\\n84|it's a personal search. Google knows your search history and it's going to show you things that it thinks you\\n85|will like. Well, chat GPT is that like times a bajillion, right? It knows who you are. you've had all these\\n86|conversations with it and you're not going to open up chat GPT and type in plumber Houston, right? You're\\n87|going to open up chat GPT and say, \\\"Hey, my garbage disposal stopped working. Uh, can do you know somebody who\\n88|can try to fix it tonight?\\\" And then chat GPT is going to go read all these different websites to find someone\\n89|who can fix it tonight. So, if you have content on your website about, you know, how quickly you respond, how\\n90|you can fix garbage disposals, and maybe you have a review, even better, if you have a review on your GBP, uh,\\n91|that somebody said, \\\"Hey, I called them about a broken garbage disposal, and they were there in 3 hours.\\\" Uh,\\n92|like that those that attribute matching is incredibly important.\", \"total_lines\": 91, \"file_size\": 9929, \"truncated\": false, \"is_binary\": false, \"is_image\": false}", "tool_call_id": "call_5430a478bc114b0b896ae921", "tool_calls": null, "tool_name": "read_file", "effect_disposition": null, "timestamp": 1787627913.197279, "token_count": null, "finish_reason": null, "reasoning": null, "reasoning_content": null, "reasoning_details": null, "codex_reasoning_items": null, "codex_message_items": null, "platform_message_id": null, "observed": 0, "active": 1, "compacted": 0}, {"id": 445, "session_id": "20260825_100001_62bb70", "role": "tool", "content": "{\"content\": \"1| So telling chat GPT who your service is not for uh is incredibly helpful at getting chat GPT to recognize,\\n2|hey, the per my user I know that my user is none of these things. So this seems like would be a good fit for\\n3|my user. >> And so you're finding that the SEO that you're doing is getting recommended is getting your\\n4|clients recommended a lot within LLMs. >> Yeah. So um so we're triing a bunch of that trust content before we\\n5|roll it out as our normal process. Of course we trial it uh what's the right system to put in place to write\\n6|the content in such a way that it gets picked up. Uh but honestly on top of that there are a couple of other\\n7|things that we started doing regularly. Uh one of them for all of our clients we do a monthly Google news\\n8|press release. Um just a press release to make sure it gets picked up by Google News. Um, we knew the most was\\n9|valuable. What are you using for press releases? >> We're using a service. It's called PR Underground. It's\\n10|the least expensive one we found that regularly lands on Google News and that's all we care about. Like if it\\n11|lands on Google News, I want the cheapest service. That's what I'm trying to get. Um, so yeah, we knew that\\n12|helped with Google rank. Uh, but you know, it's not super inexpensive. It's, you know, $80 for each one. So\\n13|for most local businesses, we didn't need to do that. Uh but we find that if they're regularly mentioned on\\n14|Google news sources, chat GPT is much more likely to pick it up. Um so we'll do that every month for every\\n15|client now. We add it like again helpful on Google, but uh we started doing it for chat GPT. And then for\\n16|clients in larger areas uh like larger metropolitan areas, we use another service. Um, boy, I think it's P R A\\n17|SAP, like press release ASAP, and they have the ability to get you into a lot of local publications like you\\n18|can publish on the, you know, whatever the local Kansas City new newspaper is. You can pay like a couple\\n19|thousand dollars and get a feature article in the local Kansas City newspaper. And again, it's helpful for\\n20|Google rank. It's even more helpful to get recommended by the AIS. Um, so that's why we started adding stuff\\n21|like that. But I mean, it's all local SEO. Like there's nothing that we do for AI that doesn't also help\\n22|Google rank. >> Huh. Right. >> Are there any are there any uh local marketing tactics outside of SEO that you\\n23|just really love? >> No. Okay. No, cuz I like sometimes I see I see just local marketing in general that I\\n24|think is so cool. Like you have like branding for example and you have like consistent branding and you have\\n25|these big bright red Vans for example and the way that the the way that your technicians dress and uh and the\\n26|way that that people behave or talk on sales call like all of that stuff you know. >> Yeah you're right. uh a\\n27|lot of the branding is very cool and you know meeting people's needs when they call you on the phone like\\n28|being nice to them and uh because Google is tracking your calls right half of the US smartphone market is\\n29|Android it's not like Google created Android out of the goodness of their heart like that data feeds the\\n30|Google search engine to make local search better so we've seen this over and over again where if you know say\\n31|one of our plumbers goes on vacation and doesn't answer the phone for 2 weeks their rank map looks noticeably\\n32|worse after two weeks of not answering the phone. uh because Google is you know we call it goal completion and\\n33|it's something that Google has been doing for over 10 years especially in the non-local space but even in the\\n34|local space if you know uh the uh for non-local the easy thing to understand is ping ponging right so you you\\n35|type something into Google you click on the third result you hit back you click on the second result then you\\n36|close your browser right that's a really bad sign for the third result because you didn't meet the searcher's\\n37|needs the same thing is true for local business, somebody calls you and you know you were really mean or rude\\n38|to them or whatever and then they hang up and then they call the second competitor like one of the other GBP\\n39|results, that's a bad sign for you because Google knows, hey, this user had to call someone else after they\\n40|called you. So 100% like when when we bring on a local client, one of their contractual agreements that we put\\n41|is that they have to have a minimum uh pickup rate where they can't have too many missed calls because we\\n42|can't rank someone who's not going to answer the phone. Um and not just because, you know, the ROI is really\\n43|hard if they don't answer the phone. That's obviously true also, but it's more that like you just can't\\n44|consistently sit in the top three if you don't answer the phone. and answer the phone like in a real customer\\n45|service ccentric sort of way. Yeah. So, I I would consider, you know, being nice to someone on the phone,\\n46|making sure they don't call one of your competitors after they get off the phone with you. That is 100% local\\n47|SEO. Um, yeah. So, and and I mean, there are cool things that a lot of people have done in the local space.\\n48|Uh, you know, the the Google uh uh LSA ads, like those are very cool. uh we don't do them and I don't know how\\n49|to do them. Um whenever a client asks, I usually refer them to someone else. Who do you refer them to? But\\n50|yeah, I mean there are a lot of uh you know there a lot of really cool Facebook ads that I've seen uh local\\n51|businesses run that have really helped to get uh business, but again we don't >> who do you refer who do you\\n52|refer them to for LSAs? >> Uh we have a I have a couple of uh other agency owners who focus on on those\\n53|aspects who don't do SEO as much. they'll focus more on paid traffic. >> How do you >> because it's also very\\n54|different like one of the challenges >> um so a lot of local business owners if they haven't done a lot of\\n55|marketing before they're used to referral traffic uh and when somebody calls you as a referral it's a very\\n56|different conversation than when someone calls you as hey they found your GBP online and then it's another\\n57|step change in how challenging the conversation is if they found you through an ad. So one of the challenges\\n58|with running ads is to have success with running ads. What we have found and it's not true for everyone of\\n59|course but for a lot of local businesses they need support and help in terms of you know how to handle a sales\\n60|call, how to handle follow-up, which is important for organic but less so and not important at all for\\n61|referral-based but it's really hard to have a positive ROI with ads unless you know how to have a sales call.\\n62|And most of the time, you know, those meta ads are just running to a receptionist or to the business owner and\\n63|then they complain that, oh, your leads are terrible. Well, maybe, but also, you're horrible selling your\\n64|service on the phone. And it's not their fault. They've never done it before. They've never had the training\\n65|and experience to talk to someone who found them from an ad. Um, so it's usually like a much higher price\\n66|point and you need to be able to provide the coaching and listen to the sales calls and we just don't have the\\n67|um infrastructure set up to provide that. But yeah, a lot of like one of the the guys that I know that I refer\\n68|people to, he uh literally has a staff that will like he'll run paid ads and his staff will answer the phone\\n69|and and sell the the person calling and then once they're sold passes them on to the business owner. >> Um\\n70|yeah, very cool business model. He's obviously much more expensive than hiring somebody to run your ads, but\\n71|he actually gives you sold leads instead of, you know, a random call from from an ad. >> What would you say to\\n72|somebody who wanted to have an AI answer the phone? >> So, what we would say like so right now having an AI\\n73|answer the phone is better than not answering the phone. Um, and some of the AIs that can answer the phone are\\n74|are quite good. Uh but you got to watch the data, right? If if people are calling and your AI is causing\\n75|people to hang up, uh then you need to get a human to answer the phone or get a better AI because again, yes,\\n76|obviously that's costing you the customer who called, but that's also impacting your rank position. If\\n77|somebody called you from an organic listing and then hung up on you and called someone else, that's pretty\\n78|bad. So, uh, you know, if you don't want to pay for like a human to answer the phone, I get it. Humans can be\\n79|expensive, have an AI do it, but watch the data, listen to the recordings, get the make sure the AI is getting\\n80|better and better until you don't see people, you know, hanging up on your AI. >> Is there any software that\\n81|you would recommend? >> Um, we've used a handful of them. Some are are are good. uh some are less good.\\n82|Usually it comes down to the prompting uh and the information that you're giving it. So I hesitate to say,\\n83|\\\"Hey, we use this one and it's great because like so now that a lot of my leads come from my YouTube channel,\\n84|uh we service people in a wide variety of service niches and you know if we had you know whatever, pick one\\n85|HVAC, right? Um, we don't have the data to be able to set up an AI agent to answer HVAC calls. Um, I have the\\n86|data across thousands of calls that I can set up an excellent agent for plumbers because we've had so many\\n87|like well over a 100 plumbing clients. Um, so I mean that's sort of it it's really in setting it up right and\\n88|you just need data to be able to do that. So yeah, uh an HVAC person, I would say, yeah, we can't help you set\\n89|up an HVAC AI call center, but we we can help a plumber set up an AI agent because we have the data, we have\\n90|the prompting to be able to do that. Sorry, am I making sense? >> Yeah. And how do you how about uh how about\\n91|like an agency on the other side of the world or something? >> Oh, um we haven't tried that. If I'm honest,\\n92|somebody didn't answer the phone. >> Yeah, we haven't tried that.\", \"total_lines\": 91, \"file_size\": 9925, \"truncated\": false, \"is_binary\": false, \"is_image\": false}", "tool_call_id": "call_4ef58ead1e4347d3b83e199a", "tool_calls": null, "tool_name": "read_file", "effect_disposition":