ed States if they have a very thick foreign accent uh they struggle to\\n5|close calls compared to uh an equivalent plumber who doesn't have that and and you know we can lament the\\n6|state of the world but I mean that's what we see in the data. >> How do you how do you approach reviews? >>\\n7|Yeah good great question. So we used to uh so the another thing that's uh in the in the contract um we rarely\\n8|set up any sort of review management type software because the conversion rates the action rates on those are\\n9|so low. What we're always going to try to do is to get the business owner uh or the service provider from\\n10|whatever phone from whatever phone number they're used to talking to the customer on. uh we'll get them to\\n11|text the customer from that phone number and ask for a review. Um the take rate on that is like 40 to 45%\\n12|that's 10 times higher than a generic you know review management service. Um, but we did change after uh when\\n13|Ask Maps started to come out. Uh, we started to, you know, dive into it and I've seen several other data dives\\n14|on where Ask Maps is getting its attributes from. And one of those sources is of course GBP reviews, but Ask\\n15|Maps seems to care a lot about non-GBP reviews. So the two changes that we made is first instead of having our\\n16|client hey could could you you know if you enjoyed our service could you leave us a review and then the GBP\\n17|review link that's all we used to do now we try to rotate review platforms uh so a GBP review a Bing for\\n18|business review chat GPT uses Bing so the Bing for business review is good for chat GPT uh we'll do a Yelp\\n19|review we'll do an Angie's review and then we'll come back to the Google review and And on top of that, the\\n20|ask is also different. So now the ask is, hey, could you tell us about what happened and what the outcome was\\n21|instead of just could you leave a review? Because what we want the user to put in are the attributes, right?\\n22|We 