ple get caught. Review gating is against Google's\\n40|terms of service. Uh if you get caught review gating, they'll typically scrub all of your reviews, which is\\n41|pretty brutal. Uh and again, it's hard to get caught. A lot of people still do this, but you know, we we try\\n42|not to do something risky like that. So, we don't review gate. And it's almost funny, right? So we've seen\\n43|that the best conversion rates uh generally happen when your rating is between like 47 and 49. Uh a perfect 50\\n44|rating generally has fewer calls. So I have given advice to clients before to like be really mean to the next\\n45|few people who walk in. Like you have a perfect 50 rating that's too good. We need to get some onestar reviews\\n46|cuz you'll get better conversions. So, just be really mean to the next few people who come in to get get some\\n47|one-star reviews. So, >> if you get bad reviews, that's part of doing business. Just get more good reviews. >>\\n48|Yeah. Um, and then I mentioned the review management software, which yeah, it's easy, but the action rates on\\n49|those are generally like 5%. Uh, so you just need, yeah, just have whatever phone number the customer is used\\n50|to dealing with, have that phone number, text them and and ask for a review. You'll get 10 times as many\\n51|reviews. >> Did you see how uh Google is asking, does this business offer rewards in exchange for reviews? >>\\n52|I did see that. Yeah, 100. And obviously that's also against their terms of service. So, uh, a lot of, you\\n53|know, I've been to a restaurant and they'll be like, \\\"Hey, if you leave us a review, we'll give you a free\\n54|bowl of queso.\\\" >> Yeah. >> Yeah. And you got to be careful with that because if if too many people say yes,\\n55|then uh, you know, Google might come and remove a lot of your reviews. >> So, you're also you you also talk\\n56|about uh about how to build up an agency, right? >> Yeah. Sometimes. Uh, so if somebody lost all of their\\n57|clients tomorrow, how would they rebuild, Caleb? >> Yeah. Uh, good question. So, a lot of that depends on how\\n58|fast they're trying to rebuild, right? So, I know this sounds kind of silly, right? But the best way to get\\n59|clients in my experience is to have started a YouTube channel like 2 years ago. Uh, and you know, obviously\\n60|starting today is better than not starting, but it takes time to build up. I'm sure. Yeah, you've uh you've\\n61|been there, Edward. It takes time. Uh, but when it starts working, it starts working like, you know, nothing\\n62|else that I' I'd ever tried. >> So, beyond that, uh, you know, going to every single Chamber of Commerce\\n63|meeting, um, not trying to sell anything. >> That's huge. But the people who attend chamber meetings are\\n64|typically local business owners and it's typically the same group of people who attend over and over again. So\\n65|just go listen to them talk about their business. Everyone loves talking about their business. Um maybe run\\n66|rank maps, email them rank maps. Uh uh email them, hey, I got a couple of ideas. Change your title tag, change\\n67|your H1. Um so many local business owners. So, we uh I have this series on my channel where I do like SEO in\\n68|real life. So, I'll literally pull up Google, I'll do a Google search, and then I'll just talk about usually\\n69|the number one ranked business, the number four ranked business, the first loser, and then somebody who's\\n70|ranked in the 20 plus range. And especially like the people who are ranked on the 20 plus range, the most\\n71|common title tag I see is the word home because that's the default title tag for so many website builders uh\\n72|for the homepage. And literally if you run a rank map and then change the title tag to be, you know, plumber\\n73|Houston instead of home, you're go and then rerun the uh rank map, you'll see improvement with that 30-\\n74|secondond shift. Um so just make friends with the people who attend. talk to them about how you do SEO, give\\n75|them free advice, you know, make these changes, do these things. And again, it takes time, but you will be\\n76|able to grow a local SEO business doing that. Um, and I suppose a third way, when we started in local SEO, we\\n77|just started running ma ads. Um, but then, you know, you need an ad budget. Uh, we probably had spent 5 10k in\\n78|ads before we had developed an offer that worked. Um I had a sales staff like people who are answering the\\n79|phone taking these calls. Uh obviously if you do the calls yourself you can avoid that that cost. Uh but it\\n80|takes a huge amount of time right like my my sales guy he was doing you know four or five calls a day um every\\n81|day six days a week for months. Um, so just it's it it gets challenging, it gets difficult, but you know, we\\n82|landed uh once we came up with an offer that works. Uh, we landed like 97 plumbers in the next 6 months. Um,\\n83|so meta ads can work, but you have to budget for it. You have to do your split testing. You have to figure out\\n84|the offer. Uh, overall just, you know, being present, you know, building actual relationships, uh, where\\n85|business owners go. Uh, that's probably the approach I would take if I were starting over because assuming,\\n86|you know, starting over, you might not have 10K to drop on split testing meta ads to find something that\\n87|works. And, you know, it's even training on yourself to get better on the phone, right? I talked about how\\n88|hard it is to close people who call from an ad. And that goes for the agency owner, too. You have to learn how\\n89|to get better on the phone. You have to listen back to your phone conversations. Um, you know, now you can\\n90|give transcripts to AI and ask AI for advice. You know, what did I do wrong? What did I do well? What do I\\n91|need to improve on? Um, all of these types of aspects. Yeah. >> Let me ask you, what what do you think\\n92|separates a sixf figureure SEO agency from a sevenfigure agency from an 8 figureure agency? >> Yeah. So,\\n93|interesting.\", \"total_lines\": 92, \"file_size\": 9984, \"truncated\": false, \"is_binary\": false, \"is_image\": false}", "tool_call_id": "call_703c0d3dea2c4d52ac753b5e", "tool_calls": null, "tool_name": "read_file", "effect_disposition": null, "timestamp": 1787627918.723848, "token_count": null, "finish_reason": null, "reasoning": null, "reasoning_content": null, "reasoning_details": null, "codex_reasoning_items": null, "codex_message_items": null, "platform_message_id": null, "observed": 0, "active": 1, "compacted": 0}, {"id": 448, "session_id": "20260825_100001_62bb70", "role": "tool", "content": "{\"content\": \"1| So, six figures you can usually get to five or six clients, right? I mean, that's $8,300 a month. Um, so a\\n2|six-figure SEO agency is very easily can be a oneperson show. You know, maybe they have a developer or\\n3|something like that or a VA, uh, but, you know, 90% one-person show. And how they got clients, you know,\\n4|referrals, the chamber of commerce stuff I talked about, you know, just very local serving their local\\n5|community, uh, doing the SEO things, right? So, um, getting to six figures is really about learning how to be\\n6|consistent with prospecting and learning how to get better on the on the phone and and closing sales. So, now\\n7|growing from 6 to 7, obviously, massive difference. Now, you need $83,000 a month. So, if your client is on\\n8|average 2K, you need what? 42 clients in order to hit that figure. So going from six to seven uh is learning\\n9|how to grow a team, right? Who do you hire? How do you hire? How do you train people? Uh like you figured out\\n10|how to prospect. That's how you got to six figures. Now you need to bring a team on board and prospect even\\n11|more, but have your team do a lot of the prospecting. Have your team manage clients. have your team uh do a\\n12|lot of the fulfillment and going to eight figures. I don't know, Edward. I'm I'm not there yet. >> Be there\\n13|soon. >> Maybe. Um but yeah, so growing above seven figures, a lot of it has just been, you know, how to make\\n14|sure that you're not the bottleneck, right? And because even at even at eight figures with 40 odd clients, you\\n15|can still be involved with every single client. You can't talk to all of them. Uh obviously, but you can still\\n16|be involved. You can you can be at the strategy meetings, you can review what you know the plan is for every\\n17|single one of them over the next month. But as you start to grow 50, 60, 70 clients, you just you suddenly\\n18|have dozens of clients that you've never even heard of the client before, right? you you have no idea what's\\n19|happening and your team needs to be just that much better to be able to manage them without even you knowing\\n20|that they're a client of yours. >> Last question, Caleb, and thank you again. You are awesome. I hope we do\\n21|many more of these episodes. This was so much fun. Uh last question. 5 years from now, what will the best SEO\\n22|agencies be doing that the average agency isn't even thinking about today? So, okay, great question. I have to\\n23|start by saying, you know, I mentioned the Sundar Pachai interview from a couple of months ago. One of the\\n24|things he said in that interview, which was mind-blowing to me, is that, you know, obviously Google used to\\n25|plan 5 10 years out into the future, but they don't do that anymore. Now, Google's long-term planning is 12\\n26|months in the future because AI is changing so much that when they plan more than 12 months out, they have to\\n27|change the plan anyway because AI did X, Y, and Z and it got this much better. So, that changes their, you\\n28|know, their two, three, four year projections. So, my first answer is, man, Google's worth $3 trillion and\\n29|they can't plan more than a year out. You want me to plan five? Um but beyond that, like even thinking 12\\n30|months, 18 months, 24 months, um it really feels like going toward a world where websites aren't visited by\\n31|people very much anymore. Um that right now we write websites for Google's algorithm, we write websites for\\n32|the LLMs, we write websites for actual people. And it feels like that's going to collapse to where we're just\\n33|going to be writing websites for AI agents. And uh the AI agent is going to get whatever relevant information\\n34|they need and then give that information back to the user. Uh so everything that we'll be doing is going to be\\n35|optimized around AI agents. uh being able to easily see, understand and regurgitate the information that we're\\n36|generating which you know uh quite different from writing websites for people and I've heard people talk about\\n37|you know websites being like infinitely customizable uh we're seeing some of that now where you can build AI\\n38|generated websites uh that serves as your Facebook landing page and then based on you know you can set you set\\n39|up Facebook ad campaigns that are running only to a very specific demographic and then you set up your landing\\n40|page to be focused on that demographic. Um, so that like ratcheting up so that websites are more and more\\n41|custom, but you know that seems like a stepping stone to people just not visiting websites anymore. Um, so I\\n42|think that's going to be largely what we look at in the future cuz and I had a conversation with someone else\\n43|and they said, \\\"Man, do you really think SEO is just going to be content and mentions in 5 years?\\\" And if you\\n44|step back, right? So if you're ever really bored, uh, having trouble falling asleep, uh, Larry Pa's original\\n45|PhD dissertation from 1999 is available online. it's public and you can read it. And he sets out the\\n46|foundation of Google search algorithm. And we all know this today. It seems obvious, but the incredible uh\\n47|flash of genius that he had was to use links as a sign of your how quality your website is, right? Uh more\\n48|links from quality websites means your website must be more quality. Quality websites don't link to spam. Uh\\n49|so that obviously is the foundation of Google search algorithm. Humorously they tried to sell that algorithm\\n50|to Excite for like $150,000 and Excite turned them down. Um anyway, so um from that foundation, Google has\\n51|spent the last 27 years trying to because you they they work hard to get their own users, right? Chat, GPT,\\n52|Claude, Google, they work hard to get their users. So now you have to build a machine that can recommend a\\n53|trustworthy business to its user based on nothing but what that machine can find online. So yes, SEO is going\\n54|to optimizing for those machines, whatever machine it is, whether you know, and I don't subscribe to GEO, AEO,\\n55|whatever acronym soup you want. I I I it's all search optimization. Whether it's optimizing for the LLMs or\\n56|for Google or for Bing, it's all optimizing for search. And for all of those tools, they're all trying to\\n57|figure out how can I decide whether this is a trustworthy business based on nothing except what I can find\\n58|online about that business cuz that's how they work. That's all that they can work on. So even in 5 years the\\n59|question is how do we optimize so that your online presence is trustworthy enough that whatever that machine\\n60|whatever you want to call that model that one is recommending you because of how it sees your online presence.\\n61|I don't know. I don't know. >> That's a great answer. I I I want to ask aside from the backlink stuff that you\\n62|talked about, are do you go hard with digital PR and yeah, you talked about press releases, you talked about\\n63|backlinks, but are you doing any anything else? >> Yeah. So, you know, so the the PR underground for the\\n64|monthly Google news reports and then the PAP, uh they're a little bit well, not a little bit, they're quite a\\n65|bit more expensive. Uh, but for the larger metros