 a typical local business. Claims that \"in 80% of markets, this alone will substantially lift rankings and call volume.\"\n\n2. **GBP category optimization** — Google allows up to 10 categories. The myth that adding secondary categories dilutes the primary category's power — he says he's tested this repeatedly and has only seen positive effects. Services under categories: don't keyword-stuff synonyms (entities, not keywords). Fill in every field, including holiday hours (only a few holidays can be entered at a time, so needs to be re-entered every few months).\n\n3. **URL structure doesn't matter much** — Google builds a picture via internal links, not URL structure. Slug = service entity is desirable (because naked-URL links put the keyword in the anchor text). Title tag most important, then H1, then first paragraph (mention the target early on). Don't start the first paragraph with \"history of the business.\"\n\n4. **Rank maps** — Grid-based local rank tracking. Metric: top-3 percent. Position 4 = \"first loser, invisible.\" Size the rank map so top competitors land at 60–90% top-3. Goal: reach at least the competitor's level, never promise more than what's been demonstrated. Tools: LeadSnap (with citation API).\n\n5. **Topical relevance threshold** — When the client's top-3 rate is roughly half of the market leader's, topical relevance is established. Then move on to geographic relevance.\n\n6. **Four types of local content** — (a) category content, (b) service page content, (c) topical relevance content, (d) geographic relevance content. In May (2026? or early 2025 — he says \"early May when Google started mass-deindexing AI slop\"), three types were unaffected. Some geo content got deindexed. Fixed by adding unique research (census data, Reddit, CRM conversations) to make it \"informationally additive.\"\n\n7. **Hyper-local landmark content** — In rank map cells where you're 4th–6th, write content about landmarks in that area (neighborhoods, lakes, zoos, city halls, universities). Verify landmarks are recognized via the Google Places API. Examples: \"plumber in River Oaks,\" \"near Montrose,\" \"near 610 West and I-10.\"\n\n8. **AI era / agentic search** — Cites a Sundar Pichai interview: Google is evolving into a \"home for agentic search.\" Websites are written for AI agents. Pichai said Google now plans on a 12-month horizon (because AI changes too fast). Attribute matching: AI looks for precise attributes (e.g., \"lead main drain line replacement\" — Boston's suburbs have lead pipes, California doesn't). \"Open-book test\": search Google/ChatGPT for a target entity, look at the favorite pages, and beat them.\n\n9. **Video for Gemini** — Ask Maps launched in March (Gemini in Google Maps). Claims the map pack algorithm \"hasn't changed meaningfully in over 10 years.\" Gemini is the only frontier model natively trained on video (owns YouTube). Publishes YouTube videos for 1/3 to 1/2 of articles, embeds them in articles. Uses Pictory for AI video. \"Nobody watches. It's not for people, it's for Gemini.\"\n\n10. **Links** — Chambers of commerce (joined 17 for a New Orleans personal injury lawyer, every one within 80 miles), youth sports sponsorships, local charities. A Gemini prompt to find sponsorship opportunities. UT Austin TEDx sponsorship for $250 → .edu link → rankings jumped 3–4 \"digits\" overnight.\n\n11. **Blog content waste** — Informational non-local blogs (e.g., \"5 ways to winterize your water heater\") don't build topical relevance. Lasik case study: 80k/month traffic from eye-health info articles but ugly rank map. Rewrote the homepage for Chicago, built geo relevance. Traffic dropped to 30–40k but rank map is all green, calls coming in.\n\n12. **Trust content (a 5th content type, in testing)** — For AI agents that read everything: pricing info, stories of what went wrong, who the service is not for. ChatGPT personalization (\"times bajillion\" personal search). Attribute matching to conversational queries (\"garbage disposal stopped working, someone tonight who can fix it?\").\n\n13. **Press releases for AI** — Monthly Google News press releases via PR Underground ($80 each) — chosen because they reliably land on Google News. Being regularly mentioned in Google News sources → ChatGPT is much more likely to pick it up. PRAsap/Press Release ASAP for placement in local newspapers ($2,000 feature articles). \"There's nothing we do for AI that doesn't also help Google rankings.\"\n\n14. **Call answering affects rankings** — Google tracks calls (Android market share), \"goal completion,\" pogo-sticking analogy: a caller who calls you and then calls a competitor = bad signal. Claims clients who don't answer the phone for two weeks see worse rank maps. Contract clause: minimum answer rate. AI phone answering is better than no answer, but watch the hang-up data.\n\n15. **Reviews** — Direct text-message requests from a familiar phone number → 40–45% response rate, vs ~5% for review management software (\"10x\"). Post-Ask Maps: rotate review platforms (GBP, Bing Places — because ChatGPT uses Bing, Yelp, Angi). The ask: \"Tell us what happened and how it turned out\" (to elicit attributes) — can't ask for specifics (Google policy). Fake reviews are illegal (FTC), review gating violates ToS and gets reviews deleted. Best conversion at rating 4.7–4.9. A perfect 5.0 gets fewer calls. Review incentives against ToS (\"Does this business offer rewards for reviews?\" prompt).\n\n16. **Multi-location** — One GBP landing page per location. Don't move a GBP from homepage to internal page while ranking (rankings plummet) — check the rank map first. After 4–5 locations the domain authority is enough that new location pages rank without a full Core 30.\n\n17. **AI images** — Real images preferred. AI images better than nothing. Add EXIF/geodata metadata to AI images to make them look real. Jay Huntley's study showed EXIF data has no effect — Edward counters this, Caleb says it's \"to make the AI images more convincing.\"\n\n18. **Future (5 years out)** — Sites are no longer visited by humans. Optimized for AI agents. Trustworthiness based on what a machine can find online. Mentions + content. Linkable assets: stunts, research reports, newsworthy stuff. Local newspapers will pick up a Huff Post mention.\n\n19. **Agency growth** — 6 digits = 5–6 clients, solo. 7 digits = 40 clients, $83k/month, team. (Business advice, not ranking technique.)\n\n20. **Ads/sales** — Meta ads need sales-call coaching, etc. LSAs are out of scope. Not a ranking technique.\n\nNow, the fact-check items against trusted sources. Let me evaluate claim by claim:\n\n**Strongly supported:**\n- Google's local ranking is based on relevance, distance, prominence — Google's own docs (Search Central's \"Optimize your local presence\"). GBP completeness matters. ✓\n- Up to 10 categories per GBP — correct, Google allows 1 primary + up to 9 secondary. ✓\n- Holiday hours need periodic updates — correct, Google only allows entering the upcoming holidays. ✓\n- Reviews matter for local rankings — supported by the Local Search Ranking Factors survey (Whitespark): review quantity/quality/recency is a top-5 local pack factor. ✓\n- Fake reviews illegal — FTC rule banning fake reviews (effective October 2024). ✓ Correct.\n- Review gating against Google's policy — correct. Google prohibits selectively soliciting only positive reviews. ✓\n- Title tag / H1 importance — standard, well-supported. ✓\n- Internal linking > URL structure — Google has stated URL structure is mostly cosmetic (\"a site's URL structure should be as simple as possible\"… John Mueller has repeatedly said URLs don't matter much for rankings). Mostly accurate. ✓\n- GBP reviews on other platforms also matter — Bing Places, Yelp etc. do feed into various systems. Plausible but weaker evidence. ChatGPT using Bing is partially correct — ChatGPT's browsing has used Bing. OpenAI partnered with Microsoft. ✓ Plausible.\n\n**Plausible but weakly evidenced / his own testing:**\n- Core 30 / website mirroring GBP — plausible mechanism (signal consistency, entity matching), but no independent study. His own split tests. This is actually close to best-practice consensus: category-linked pages are standard local SEO (the \"GBP landing page\" concept is standard).\n- Secondary categories don't dilute — consistent with most practitioners' findings (e.g., Darren Shaw / Whitespark data suggests category selection is a top ranking factor and adding appropriate categories is beneficial).\n- Rank maps / top-3 metric — standard practice.\n- Topical relevance threshold = half of leader — his own heuristic, unverified externally.\n- Hyper-local landmark content — mixed. This is a known tactic with risks: Google's spam policies on \"scaled content\" and doorway pages. He acknowledges deindexing happened and says he fixed it with additive research. Honest.\n- EXIF on AI images — Jay Huntley's study found no effect. Edward agrees. Caleb does it anyway. Honest disagreement, low evidence.\n- Press releases → ChatGPT pickup — anecdotal, no independent evidence. Plausible via RAG/web crawl but unproven. PR Underground landing on Google News is also questionable (Google News inclusion has gotten stricter).\n\n**Dubious / probably wrong or hype:**\n1. **\"Google tracks calls and uses them as ranking signals… goal completion… if you don't answer the phone for two weeks your rank map gets worse\"** — This is the most dubious claim. No credible evidence that Google uses call-answer behavior from Android phones as a local ranking signal. \"Goal completion\" is a concept from the non-local space (task completion) — Google has confirmed using click-through/pogo-stick-type signals in various forms, but phone-call tracking via Android as a ranking factor is speculation. Google has denied using such data. This is a practitioner myth with confirmation bias (businesses that don't answer the phone are often low-quality businesses in general). Flag: unproven, probably confounded.\n2. **\"Map pack algorithm hasn't changed meaningfully in 10 years\"** — Overstatement. The core 3-factor framework (relevance/distance/prominence) has been stable, but there have been meaningful changes: review handling updates, spam-fighting updates, 2021–2023 local updates, the introduction of GBP performance metrics, etc. \"Hasn't changed meaningfully\" is hyperbole. Partially true as a framework.\n3. **\"Gemini is the only frontier model natively trained on video\"** — Misleading/wrong. GPT-4o is natively multimodal including video. Claude has video support. Saying Gemini is the only one \"natively trained on video\" is false. GPT-4o was natively multimodal from day one. The \"Gemini loves video\" conclusion is partly true (video-rich training data via YouTube), but \"the only frontier model\" is a factual error.\n4. **Pichai claims** — The agentic search direction is real (Google's AI Mode, Pichai has talked about agentic). The \"planning is now 12 months instead of 5–10 years\" claim — I recall Pichai saying something similar at an earnings call (something like \"in this era… we plan on a shorter horizon\"). Might be checkable. The gist is plausible.\n5. **\"Google works on entities, not keywords\"** — Common SEO shorthand. Partially true (Knowledge Graph, entity understanding), but keywords still matter as signals. Overstated, but directionally fine.\n6. **UT Austin TEDx .edu link → rankings \"3–4 digits overnight\"** — Anecdotal, possibly true for that client. No problem.\n7. **\"90–95% of local traffic still comes from the map pack\"** — Probably wrong/exaggerated. Map pack gets a large share of local clicks, but organic results still get significant share. Studies (e.g., various CTR studies) show map pack ~30–40% of local clicks, organic ~50–60% depending on query. His figure looks inflated. Also contradicts the premise of his own Core 30 (organic matters). Flag.\n8. **\"Ask Maps launched in March\"** — Google la