icts himself by adding metadata anyway. Note this internal contradiction.\n- Claim that \"Google is entity-based and doesn't use keywords anymore\" — oversimplified. Google uses entities but keywords still matter.\n- Claim that \"Google tracks calls via Android data and ranks you lower if you don't answer the phone\" — this is an unverified hypothesis. Google denies using call data as a ranking factor. No official confirmation exists. Local Search Ranking Factors studies rank reviews/GBP signals as high. The call-answer-rate claim is speculative — flag as \"unproven.\"\n- Claim that \"90–95% of local traffic still comes from the Map Pack\" — that's his agency's observation, not a general statistic.\n- Bing for Business reviews being used by ChatGPT — plausible given ChatGPT's browsing uses Bing. The local impact of Bing Places is real. But it's not officially confirmed that ChatGPT specifically uses Bing for Business reviews. Flag as \"plausible but unverified.\"\n- \"4.7–4.9 rating converts better than 5.0\" — consistent with a well-known study (a 2019 Northwestern University study found that conversion peaks around 4.2–4.5. Another study showed the effect of \"perfect\" ratings). Plausible.\n- Pictory for AI video — no issue.\n- PR Underground / Press Release ASAPP — these are paid placement services. Using them itself isn't a problem, but the \"Google News pickup\" claim is a vendor claim.\n\nAlso, his \"Core 30\" approach — a website that mirrors GBP categories/services. This aligns with standard best practices (category page structure, entity consistency) and matches the GEO/entity-first direction Eitan is aiming for.\n\nKey fact-checks I should do:\n1. Existence and timing of Ask Maps.\n2. May 2025 deindexing of AI content (Sterling Sky Davis / Search Engine Land).\n3. Pichai's agentic-search remarks + 12-month planning.\n4. Whitespark's Local Search Ranking Factors (proximity/categories/reviews as top factors).\n5. Secondary categories: does adding categories help, or does it dilute? Community consensus (Mike Blumenthal, Sterling Sky Davis) has found that categories do matter, but the primary category matters most. Adding secondary categories is generally not harmful.\n\nLet's try to fetch these via jina reader with actual known URLs.\n", "reasoning_content": "Search engines are blocked from this machine. Let's directly fetch primary sources that we know exist:\n\n1. Google's official local ranking factors (relevance/distance/prominence) — already confirmed above.\n2. Google's \"site reputation abuse\" / helpful content / spam policy — partially confirmed.\n3. Whitespark's Local Search Ranking Factors survey — this is a known study, and the URL is probably whitespark.ca/local-search-ranking-factors. Let's try fetching this directly.\n4. The May 2025 deindexing — there were actually reports around early May 2025 that Google had been deindexing tons of AI-generated content, especially in local SEO (Sterling Sky Davis wrote about it). Try fetching Sterling's site directly, or try Search Engine Land's site search.\n5. Ask Maps — Google announced a conversational \"Ask Maps\" with Gemini in early 2026 (I recall it was announced in March 2026). The podcast says \"rolled out in March,\" which is consistent with early 2026. Try the Google Keyword blog / Maps blog directly.\n6. Sundar Pichai's interview — there was a recent Pichai interview (Lex Fridman, or was it an Indian podcast with Nikhil Kamath?) that touched on agentic search. Actually, Pichai said Google's planning horizon has shortened at an interview — this might be from the Lex Fridman podcast (October 2025?). Let's verify via blog.google or news articles.\n\nAlso verify:\n- \"The Map Pack algorithm hasn't substantively changed in over 10 years\" — this is a common claim in the industry. The core of relevance/distance/prominence has indeed been stable. Reasonable to say \"true in essence.\"\n- EXIF/geotag study — Jay Huntley… actually, the famous study on EXIF data in