oorway-page risk is real — Google's spam policy explicitly prohibits doorway pages (thin location pages). His defense is uniqueness through research, which matches the helpful-content bar. May 2025 de-indexing anecdote: he says Google mass-deindexed \"AI slop\" in early May and their geo content took some hits; they enriched with research and got re-indexed. I couldn't verify the specific May event from here (search was blocked), but it's plausible against Google's site reputation abuse / scaled content abuse policies. Status: plausible, high-risk tactic — needs strict quality bar; exactly the kind of thing WAO would need to be careful with at scale (automating geo content for Hebrew local clients could get flagged as doorway/scaled content if not done carefully).\n\n8. EXIF/geolocation data on AI images: he admits the study (Jay Huntley's) showed EXIF doesn't matter. He still does it as a defensive measure.\n   - Judgment: consistent with the cited study (no effect). Fake EXIF on AI images is dubious and possibly deceptive; low value. Status: not evidence-backed.\n\n9. Video for Gemini: Ask Maps rollout in March; map-pack algorithm hasn't changed much in 10 years; Gemini is natively trained on video (Google owns YouTube); they publish AI-generated videos via Pictory to YouTube and embed them on articles, \"for Gemini, not humans.\"\n   - Judgment: Gemini/Ask Maps existence is real (Google has been pushing conversational features in Maps; Ask Maps/Gemini in Maps announced around March 2025 — I recall it was announced at the March 2025 Google I/O-related event or in Google Maps announcements; the transcript says \"Ask Maps launched in March\" — plausible, March 2025). \"Map pack algorithm hasn't changed in 10 years\" is overstated but reflects the practitioner sentiment that proximity/relevance/prominence still dominate. \"Gemini natively trained on video\" — Gemini is multimodal and does train on video; plausible. The tactical claim \"publish AI videos nobody watche