ls = lost revenue either way). Status: unproven but harmless to adopt as practice; shouldn't be sold as a ranking mechanism.\n\n18. AI answering phones: better than not answering; watch hang-up data.\n    - Judgment: practical advice; no ranking claims. Status: fine.\n\n19. Press releases: PR Underground ($80/mo) monthly for Google News pickup → helps ChatGPT recommendations; PR ASAP for local newspaper features ($2,000) for bigger cities.\n    - Judgment: press releases for Google News pickup = low-quality tactic; Google News inclusion doesn't mean much. Claim \"ChatGPT is much more likely to recommend\" is unverified. Buying your way into local newspaper features is more defensible (real editorial mention = entity credibility), but paid-placement disclosure issues. Status: weak-to-speculative; not recommended as a core tactic.\n\n20. LLMs favor big brands/multi-location (Roto-Rooter vs. Joe the Plumber); proximity matters less for AI recommendations than for map pack.\n    - Judgment: consistent with multiple GEO studies (LLMs recommend brands they have data on; brand mentions matter). Strategic implication: small businesses need to build online presence early. Status: plausible, supported by GEO observations.\n\n21. Sundar Pichai interview: Google planning horizons now 12 months (AI moving too fast); Google as \"home for agentic search.\"\n    - Judgment: I recall Pichai discussing AI mode/agentic search; the \"planning only 12 months out\" quote — I can't verify the exact quote from here, but it's consistent with known Pichai comments (he said something similar on Lex Fridman's podcast in 2025 — wait, the transcript references \"the interview that came out about a month ago\" and \"Lex Friedman\"… actually it says \"an interview with Sundar Pichai came out about a month ago\" — probably a mid-2025 one). Status: unverifiable from here but directionally consistent with public statements.\n\n22. SEO trajectory: websites read by AI agents, not humans; write for mach