 Core principle\\n\\nDo not trust code inspection alone. Verify the exact surface the user touches.\\n\\n## Autonomy and consent boundaries\\n\\n- Continue autonomously when the repository, configured credentials, and test fixture already provide the required context. Do not ask the user to repeat IDs, setup steps, or permission for non-destructive work.\\n- If the user completed external setup, treat it as the test fixture: wire it into the application, verify the binding, and continue to the next safe implementation step.\\n- Ask only immediately before an externally mutating operation, such as creating a Google Ads account/campaign, changing a budget, or deploying. State the exact resource and scope; do not defer the surrounding read-only work.\\n- Do not stop after identifying a missing app binding when it can be safely synchronized and verified during the current session.\\n- If the execution environment denies an externally mutating command, do not retry or bypass it. Do not make the user hunt for a generic prompt. State that no action is required unless a concrete approval UI appears, and continue all independent read-only audits, tests, and implementation work.\\n\\n## Default verification ladder\\n\\nUse the narrowest signal that proves the change:\\n\\n1. `npm run build` to catch compile/type errors in the changed seam.\\n2. `npm run dev` to boot the app when runtime behavior matters.\\n3. `curl` the route for HTTP status and emitted markup/content.\\n4. Use browser automation for one user-visible smoke test when the change is interactive.\\n5. If the repo-wide lint is noisy, run targeted `eslint` on the files you changed and treat that as supporting evidence, not the sole proof.\\n6. For page + backend integrations, verify the contract from both ends and make sure the page is reading the same durable identifier the backend writes, not a fuzzy heuristic when a stable index is available.\\n7. When a user has completed external test infrastructure (for example, a test MCC, child account, and campaign), do not hand application configuration work back to them. Inspect the durable bindings, synchronize placeholders from configured test values without exposing credentials, and read the stored binding back through the provider API.\\n8. When a feature has explicit execution modes (for example, `test` vs `live`), verify the rendered toggle/state, the forwarded request payload, and the route’s mode-specific credential or account selection logic.\\n\\nIf the change affects an API route, prefer `curl` or a direct HTTP request first. If it affects a page, confirm the rendered HTML plus one visual/browser assertion. If it affects both, do both.\\n\\nFor route/API pairs, verify the contract from both ends when the feature spans a page and a backend handler. A page render alone does not prove the API works, and a 200 from the API alone does not prove the user-facing route renders.\\n\\n## What to check\\n\\n- HTTP status is correct.\\n- The expected route renders the expected heading, CTA, or key copy.\\n- Critical scripts/links are present when needed.\\n- No blank page / redirect loop / missing data state.\\n- The page remains reachable in the browser with the intended UI shape.\\n- If the feature has sandbox/live or similar execution modes, confirm the UI shows the mode, the request forwards it, and the backend chooses the matching credential set.\\n- In Next.js 16, `src/proxy.ts` (the former middleware) runs before a page-level `redirect()`. An unauthenticated `curl -I` Location is the proxy hop, not the page gate. To observe a page-level `/admin/login?next=...` redirect, the path must not be in a proxy protected list — or the proxy itself must emit that Location. Verify both layers; do not assume the page `redirect()` is what curl sees.\\n- If the feature depends on a durable client association, verify the producer writes a client index and the dashboard reads that index before any heuristic fallback.\\n\\n## Pitfalls\\n\\n- Do not stop at lint if lint is noisy with unrelated repo-wide failures; use build + runtime smoke checks for the changed route as the proof, and call out the unrelated failures separately.\\n- Quote paths containing parentheses or other shell-special characters when running targeted commands, or the verification itself may fail before the code is even checked.\\n- Do not call a build pass sufficient for interactive features.\\n- Do not call a browser screenshot sufficient if the route itself is 500/404.\\n- Do not overfit verification to one route if the change spans a route pair (for example, page + API handler).\\n- For API routes, include at least one concrete negative case (400/404) when it documents the contract and can be exercised safely.\\n- **Israeli Legal Compliance (Privacy & Accessibility):** \\n  - Ensure client generated LPs and 5-page sites include the required concise privacy disclosure (§11 Privacy Protection Law) naming the client business as the data controller, along with a lead-form consent checkbox linking to the policy.\\n  - Accessibility micro-page (`accessibility.html`) and statement must be included unless the client is self-declared `osek_patur` or under ₪120,000/yr revenue. Floating accessibility widgets and EU cookie banners are explicitly out of scope and must not be added.\\n  - Verify accessibility/privacy links render correctly with `noindex, follow` tags.\\n  - Ensure the keyword-first title defaults to `${primaryService}${targetLocation ? ' — ' + targetLocation : ''} | ${businessName}` and Tamar's copy generator enforces primaryService presence in hero headlines for Google Ads Quality Score relevance.\\n- For dashboard pages that aggregate from filesystem artifacts, verify the dashboard plus the producer route and prefer a stable stored link/index over a heuristic match when the product depends on a durable association.\\n- When verifying end-to-end client simulations (such as generated landing pages paired with Google Ads campaigns), verify both the rendered app route (`/lp/[slug]`) and the corresponding campaign JSON/API payload structure to ensure absolute alignment of copy, USP, and Final URL.\\n- Ensure internal ops-load survey fields (like capacity units) never leak into customer-facing copy fields (such as scarcity lines).\\n- Enforce explicit channel checks for CTA button labels (preventing primary phone CTAs from accidentally mirroring WhatsApp when multiple channels are selected).\\n- When a visible demo/sandbox mode exists, make the mode obvious in the dashboard or summary view so prospects can tell they are not in a live account.\\n- Enforce explicit channel checks for CTA button labels (preventing primary phone CTAs from accidentally mirroring WhatsApp when multiple channels are selected).\\n- When a visible demo/sandbox mode exists, make the mode obvious in the dashboard or summary view so prospects can tell they are not in a live account.\\n\\n## Good evidence bundle\\n\\nWhen reporting success, capture at least:\\n- build result\\n- route/status result\\n- one browser-visible assertion for the user-facing page\\n\\n## Support files\\n\\n- `references/route-smoke-checks.md` — reusable smoke-check recipes and notes from real route verification sessions.\\n- `references/weekly-proactive-loop.md` — automated weekly proactive loops, cron route security (`CRON_SECRET`), batch error isolation, and Wizard-of-Oz client delivery patterns.\\n- `references/google-ads-durable-binding.md` — campaign/client association pattern for dashboard-backed Google Ads flows: persist a per-client index, prefer it over heuristic lookup, and verify both producer and consumer routes.\\n- `references/google-ads-sandbox-live-mode.md` — sandbox vs live Google Ads execution mode, separate manager credentials, and the onboarding toggle pattern.\\n- `references/google-ads-sandbox-verification.md` — approval-gated sandbox mutation policy, read-only Google Ads API read-back, and the evidence required before any live enablement.\\n- `references/google-ads-provisioning-validation.md` — validate-only probes and current Google Ads API field requirements for test-hierarchy campaign provisioning.\\n- `references/google-ads-provisioning-api.md` — July 2026 test-hierarchy provisioning workflow, validated conversion/campaign field mappings, durable binding checks, and production access gates.\\n- `references/google-ads-api-provisioning.md` — concise safe sequence, Google Ads API v24 field compatibility notes, and evidence required for a real test-hierarchy provision.\\n- `references/google-ads-execution-loop.md` — guidelines for implementing and verifying Google Ads mutation routes (`campaign-status`, `budget`, `negative-keywords`), executor state transitions (`executed` vs `failed`), and approval log updates.\\n- `references/production-ssh-deployment.md` — remote PM2 deployment, server-local environment handling, SSH smoke checks, and the autonomy/verification sequence.\\n\", \"path\": \"software-development/nextjs-route-verification/SKILL.md\", \"skill_dir\": \"/home/eitanya/.hermes/profiles/orchestrator/skills/software-development/nextjs-route-verification\", \"linked_files\": {\"references\": [\"references/google-ads-provisioning-api.md\", \"references/google-ads-sandbox-verification.md\", \"references/pm2-production-env.md\", \"references/google-ads-api-provisioning.md\", \"references/route-smoke-checks.md\", \"references/weekly-proactive-loop.md\", \"references/google-ads-sandbox-live-mode.md\", \"references/google-ads-execution-loop.md\", \"references/google-ads-durable-binding.md\", \"references/production-ssh-deployment.md\", \"references/google-ads-provisioning-validation.md\"]}, \"usage_hint\": \"To view linked files, call skill_view(name, file_path) where file_path is e.g. 'references/api.md' or 'assets/config.yaml'\", \"required_environment_variables\": [], \"required_commands\": [], \"missing_required_environment_variables\": [], \"missing_credential_files\": [], \"missing_required_commands\": [], \"setup_needed\": false, \"setup_skipped\": false, \"readiness_status\": \"available\", \"metadata\": {\"hermes\": {\"tags\": [\"nextjs\", \"verification\", \"smoke-test\", \"browser\", \"http\", \"app-router\"], \"related_skills\": [\"systematic-debugging\", \"test-driven-development\", \"requesting-code-review\", \"plan\"]}}}", "tool_call_id": "call_07dda766d4c94031ab293d69", "tool_calls": null, "tool_name": "skill_view", "effect_disposition": null, "timestamp": 1787584693.8699346, "token_count": null, "finish_reason": null, "reasoning": null, "reasoning_content": null, "reasoning_details": null, "codex_reasoning_items": null, "codex_message_items": null, "platform_message_id": null, "observed": 0, "active": 1, "compacted": 0}, {"id": 263, "session_id": "20260824_221719_f9c1b5", "role": "tool", "content": "{\"success\": true, \"name\": \"wao-kanban-orchestration\", \"description\": \"Dispatch and verify WAO repo work through Hermes kanban + the handoff/ directory. Covers exact dispatch command shape, worker behavioral pitfalls, independent verification protocol, and creating new agent profiles.\", \"tags\": [\"wao\", \"kanban\", \"orchestration\", \"handoff\", \"dispatch\", \"verification\"], \"related_skills\": [], \"content\": \"---\\nname: wao-kanban-orchestration\\ndescription: Dispatch and verify WAO repo work through Hermes kanban + the handoff/ directory. Covers exact dispatch command shape, worker behavioral pitfalls, independent verification protocol, and creating new agent profiles.\\nversion: 1.0.0\\ntags: [wao, kanban, orchestration, handoff, dispatch, verification]\\n---\\n\\n# WAO Kanban Orchestration\\n\\nHow the Lead Orchestrator dispatches WAO specs to sub-agent profiles (`waoengineer`,\\n`waocopy`, `waostrategy`, verifier tier) via `hermes kanban` and verifies results\\nindependently. The orchestrator plans and coordinates; it never writes production code\\nor Hebrew copy itself.\\n\\n## When to use\\n- A spec sits in `handoff/pending/` and needs to reach a worker profile.\\n- A prior dispatch died / landed in an empty scratch dir and must be re-created.\\n- You need to verify a completed kanban task before reporting it done to Eitan.\\n- You must stand up a new agent profile (new role in AGENTS.md).\\n\\n## Dispatch (exact command shape — deviations cause silent failures)\\n\\n```\\nhermes kanban create \\\"<short title>\\\" --assignee <profile> \\\\\\n  --workspace dir:/home/eitanya/wao \\\\\\n  --idempotency-key wao-<date>-<seq>-v<N> \\\\\\n  --body \\\"Your working directory is the WAO repo at /home/eitanya/wao. Read handoff/pending/<spec>.md and execute it exactly as written. <one-line scope reminder>. When done, call kanban_complete with a one-paragraph summary and the 'git status --porcelain' output. If you cannot proceed, call kanban_block with the reason. Exiting without calling one of those is a protocol failure.\\\"\\n```\\n\\nNon-negotiables:\\n- **`--workspace dir:/home/eitanya/wao`** — omit it and the worker runs in an empty\\n  scratch dir with no repo and no spec file. This is why the `swarm` pilot failed.\\n- **Explicit `kanban_complete`/`kanban_block` instruction in the body** — a worker that\\n  exits without calling one is marked crashed even if it did the work.\\n- **`--idempotency-key`** — set it on re-dispatches so a retried command returns the\\n  existing task id instead of creating a duplicate.\\n\\n**Never use `hermes kanban swarm`** — it ignores workspace config; every worker it\\nspawns lands in an empty scratch dir. Build dependency chains by hand with\\n`--parent <task_id>` per stage.\\n\\n## Re-creating specs whose earlier dispatch died\\n- First run `hermes kanban list --archived` — dead attempts (e.g. the broken swarm\\n  pilot) sit there with no deliverables; the archived list shows what NOT to trust\\n  and gives you titles to reuse.\\n- Re-create with a fresh `--idempotency-key` suffix (`-v2`, `-v3`) so the new task\\n  cannot collide with anything live.\\n- After the batch finishes, confirm `handoff/pending/` no longer contains those\\n  specs (workers forget the move — see verification step 4).\\n\\n## Pitfalls (learned in the field)\\n- **\\\"No gateway is running\\\" warning from `kanban create` is often a false positive.**\\n  Verify with `hermes gateway status` and confirm the task actually flips to `running`\\n  on the next dispatcher tick (~60s) before concluding anything is broken. Do NOT\\n  restart the gateway on the warning alone.\\n- **Workers frequently forget to move their handoff spec `pending/ → completed/`** even\\n  when the kanban task completes cleanly. The orchestrator must do this move itself\\n  during verification (see below). Check `ls handoff/pending/` after every batch.\\n- **Dispatch ≠ done.** A task showing `done` is a self-report; always verify independently.\\n- **Wait-loop expectations:** dispatcher tick ≈ 60 s; read-only research tasks ran 4–11 min\\n  each; four parallel tasks completed within ~15 min wall-clock. Poll `hermes kanban list`\\n  on 4–5 min sleeps instead of hammering it.\\n\\n## Independent verification protocol (observation, not trust)\\n1. `git status --porcelain` yourself — confirm only the expected new/modified files,\\n   no scope creep outside the spec's named paths. Flag any out-of-scope diff to Eitan.\\n2. `read_file` the actual deliverable(s) — never relay the worker's summary as proof.\\n3. For `waoengineer` reports, run the Hebrew-byte scan (workers must type ZERO Hebrew):\\n   see `references/dispatch-and-verify.md` for the one-liner.\\n4. Move the handoff spec `handoff/pending/<spec>.md → handoff/completed/` yourself\\n   (or `handoff/failed/` with the reason). Workers usually skip this.\\n5. `hermes kanban show <id>` to read the worker's completion note + run history.\\n\\n## Creating a new agent profile\\n```\\nhermes profile create <name> --clone --clone-from waoengineer \\\\\\n  --description \\\"<role summary for the kanban decomposer>\\\"\\n```\\n- Cloning inherits the working Qwen/alibaba `config.yaml` + `.env` credentials (both\\n  `QWEN_API_KEY` and `DASHSCOPE_API_KEY` are required for alibaba profiles).\\n- Before recreating a previously DELETED profile, check `~/.hermes/profiles.backup-*`\\n  and `~/.hermes/profiles.archived-*` for the old config — it records the prior\\n  model/role and prevents silently overwriting history.\\n- **`--clone` copies the SOURCE profile's SOUL.md.** You MUST rewrite\\n  `~/.hermes/profiles/<name>/SOUL.md` for the new role immediately.\\n- Verify with `hermes profile show <name>` (model + .env present) and a live smoke\\n  test: `hermes -p <name> -z \\\"Reply with exactly: OK\\\"`.\\n- Update the constitution docs (AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, CLAUDE_TO_HERMES_HANDOFF.md) to\\n  match the new architecture.\\n\\n## Quick command reference\\n- Board state: `hermes kanban list` / `--archived`\\n- Task detail + completion note: `hermes kanban show <id>`\\n- Gateway: `hermes status` or `hermes gateway status`\\n- One-shot profile probe: `hermes -p <profile> -z \\\"<prompt>\\\"` (no `--max-turns` flag)\\n\", \"path\": \"wao/wao-kanban-orchestration/SKILL.md\", \"skill_dir\": \"/home/eitanya/.hermes/profiles/orchestrator/skills/wao/wao-kanban-orchestration\", \"linked_files\": {\"references\": [\"references/dispatch-and-verify.md\"]}, \"usage_hint\": \"To view linked files, call skill_view(name, file_path) where file_path is e.g. 'references/api.md' or 'assets/config.yaml'\", \"required_environment_variables\": [], \"required_commands\": [], \"missing_required_environment_variables\": [], \"missing_credential_files\": [], \"missing_required_commands\": [], \"setup_needed\": false, \"setup_skipped\": false, \"readiness_status\": \"available\"}", "tool_call_id": "call_dcdabed7dd104f62a76325ba", "tool_calls": null, "tool_name": "skill_view", "effect_disposition": null, "timestamp": 1787584694.9074147, "token_count": null, "finish_reason": null, "reasoning": null, "reasoning_content": null, "reasoning_details": null, "codex_reasoning_items": null, "codex_message_items": null, "platform_message_id": null, "observed": 0, "active": 1, "compacted": 0}, {"id": 264, "session_id": "20260824_221719_f9c1b5", "role": "tool", "content": "{\"output\": \"M AGENTS.md\\n M CLAUDE.md\\n M CLAUDE_TO_HERMES_HANDOFF.md\\n M docs/research/site-bot/README.md\\n?? docs/research/site-bot/005_switching-cost-asset-inventory.md\\n?? docs/research/site-bot/006_onboarding-decision-set-autofill.md\\n?? docs/research/site-bot/007_referral-attribution-feasibility.md\\n?? docs/research/site-bot/008_retention-interview-script.md\\n?? docs/research/site-bot/skira-sabra-rewrite.md\\n?? docs/research/site-bot/skira-transcript.md\\n?? docs/research/site-bot/skira.pdf\\n---\\n0bbe65b chore: save state and completed research reports from Claude Code handoff\\n031d613 Fix GBP getLocation URL and add live-call gate\\n316f332 Fix YouTube embed Error 153: correct Referrer-Policy + re-upload lessons 3-5 to WAO channel\\n388df9b Fix production build break in GEO upgrade callback's background pipeline\\n828f45e Bundle GEO upgrade, Core-30 LP, review flywheel, and site-bot pivot work\\n---\\nhermes-migration\", \"exit_code\": 0, \"error\": null}", "tool_call_id": "call_a77a19ced26543b8b7114038", "tool_calls": null, "tool_name": "terminal", "effect_disposition": null, "timestamp": 1787584696.0300295, "token_count": null, "finish_reason": null, "reasoning": null, "reasoning_content": null, "reasoning_details": null, "codex_reasoning_items": null, "codex_message_items": null, "platform_message_id": null, "observed": 0, "active": 1, "compacted": 0}, {"id": 265, "session_id": "20260824_221719_f9c1b5", "role": "tool", "content": "{\"content\": \"1|@AGENTS.md\\n2|\\n3|# WAO — Project Constitution\\n4|\\n5|Shared ground truth that **every agent inherits**. Keep it tight and durable; role-specific\\n6|craft lives in `.claude/agents/*.md`. *(Seed — being formalized as we build the agent framework.)*\\n7|\\n8|## Stack & environment\\n9|- **Next.js 16.2.6** (Turbopack, App Router), React 19. Marketing site for **WAO** — a B2C\\n10|  digital-marketing agency. Hebrew, **RTL**. ~97 knowledge articles + service pages.\\n11|- **Dev server:** `npm run dev` → http://localhost:3000.\\n12|  - `next.config.ts` changes require a **server restart** to take effect.\\n13|  - If routes 404 after a hard kill of the server, the `.next` dev cache is corrupt → `rm -rf .next` and restart.\\n14|- **Deploy:** the user runs it — `ssh wao@91.98.195.242` → `cd ~/htdocs/www.wao.co.il` → `./deploy.sh`.\\n15|\\n16|## Hard constraints (violating these is a failure)\\n17|- **`src/data/knowledge.ts`:** never edit with the Write tool or free-form. **Surgical Python\\n18|  `str.replace()` only.** Copy is authored by the copywriter as exact `old → new` strings; the\\n19|  nextjs-engineer applies them.\\n20|- **SEO keyword anchors** — page `<title>`, `<h1>`, hero badge head terms — never change without\\n21|  sign-off from the seo-strategist.\\n22|\\n23|## RTL / Hebrew rendering\\n24|- **Article body bidi** is handled by `renderMixed()` in `knowledge/[slug]/page.tsx`: it wraps\\n25|  Latin runs in `<bdi dir=\\\"ltr\\\">` and leaves brackets in the RTL flow so mixed-script parentheses\\n26|  mirror correctly. Regression check — must be 0:\\n27|  `grep -oP '<bdi dir=\\\"ltr\\\">\\\\([^)<]*</bdi>'` on a rendered page.\\n28|- **Meta-title / SERP bidi rule:** a Hebrew `<title>` must never end in a bare Latin token\\n29|  immediately followed by the Latin brand — they bidi-swap in Google's RTL results\\n30|  (`…ל-SEO | WAO` → `…ל-WAO | SEO`). Enforced by: (a) root template anchors the brand with an\\n31|  RLM — `\\\"%s‏ | WAO\\\"`; (b) titles stay concise (no redundant double-suffix) so Google keeps ours.\\n32|  **Final confirmation needs a live SERP / Search-Console re-check after deploy** — it can't be\\n33|  validated locally. Owners: **ux** (bidi technique) + **nextjs-engineer** (template);\\n34|  **seo-strategist** owns the title formula; **language-qa** is the SERP gate that catches it.\\n35|- **Typography:** em-dash ( — ) single-spaced; Hebrew gershayim (״)/geresh (׳), never ASCII\\n36|  `\\\"`/`'`; no double spaces. Owner: language-qa.\\n37|\\n38|## Copy & voice\\n39|- **Reader:** intelligent Israeli B2C business owner — ROI-minded, busy, allergic to jargon and\\n40|  to translated-from-English Hebrew.\\n41|- **Voice:** elite consultant over coffee — analogy-first, scannable, flawless native Hebrew.\\n42|  Full brief in `.claude/agents/copywriter.md`.\\n43|- **Positioning is per-page** (e.g. \\\"outsourced marketing managers\\\" on `/consulting`) — never\\n44|  globalized across the site without explicit intent.\\n45|\\n46|## Agents\\n47|Specialists live in `.claude/agents/`. Orchestration via **\\\"Adam\\\"** (design TBD). Chartered so\\n48|far: **copywriter** (Tamar), **language-qa** (Noa), **seo-strategist** (Yonatan), **ux** (Maya),\\n49|**ppc-strategist** (Dror), **nextjs-engineer** (Eitan-Dev), **verifier** (Roni), **instructional-designer** (Gil).\\n50|Orchestration via **Adam** — see below.\\n51|\\n52|## Orchestration (Adam)\\n53|\\n54|**Adam is the main session, not a subagent.** Subagents can't call each other or sub-delegate —\\n55|they run isolated and report only to their caller. So *all* cross-agent flow goes through Adam: he\\n56|is the conductor and the bus. \\\"Talk to Adam\\\" = talk to the main Claude Code session in orchestrator\\n57|mode. (There is deliberately no `adam.md` — a subagent couldn't spawn the others.)\\n58|\\n59|**Interaction model — hybrid:**\\n60|- Multi-step or unsure who's needed → **Adam** plans, delegates, routes, and synthesizes.\\n61|- A known single task → call the specialist directly (still bound by the seams/gates below).\\n62|\\n63|**How Adam runs a mission:**\\n64|1. Read the request + this file + the relevant charters in `.claude/agents/`.\\n65|2. Plan the stages and owners. Known mission → follow the documented pipeline in `docs/missions/`.\\n66|   One-off → plan ad-hoc from the charters.\\n67|3. Delegate one stage at a time; fan out **independent** stages in parallel, **dependent** stages\\n68|   sequentially. Pass each specialist exactly what they need.\\n69|4. Collect output → **enforce the gate** → route to the next owner.\\n70|5. Report back: what each did, what's verified, what's left.\\n71|\\n72|**Seams & gates Adam enforces (non-negotiable):**\\n73|- Any copy/script → **language-qa (Noa)** before it ships.\\n74|- `knowledge.ts` → **nextjs-engineer (Eitan-Dev)** only, Python `str.replace`, asserted counts.\\n75|- Keyword anchors / title formula → **seo-strategist (Yonatan)** sign-off.\\n76|- SERP / RTL bidi → **ux (Maya)** technique + Eitan-Dev template; Noa is the SERP gate.\\n77|- Time-sensitive SEO/PPC claims → strategist **web-verifies + dates** them (never from memory).\\n78|- Anything built → **verifier (Roni)** confirms at runtime before \\\"done.\\\" No partial pass.\\n79|\\n80|**Models:** each specialist runs on its **pinned** model regardless of the session model — that's\\n81|the guarantee against language-quality regressions. Documented pipelines live in `docs/missions/`.\\n82|\\n83|# Orchestrator Instructions (Strategist = Hermes profile `waostrategy`, Qwen 3.8 Max)\\n84|\\n85|Before doing ANY work in this repository, read these two files:\\n86|- AGENTS.md\\n87|- CLAUDE_TO_HERMES_HANDOFF.md\\n88|\\n89|## Your Role\\n90|You are the Strategist (Hermes profile: `waostrategy`, model qwen3.8-max via DashScope).\\n91|You THINK and PLAN. You do NOT write production code.\\n92|Execution is done by the Hermes execution profiles (`waoengineer`, `waocopy`, verifier tier).\\n93|\\n94|## How You Pass the Stick\\n95|1. Break the mission into small, single-purpose tasks.\\n96|2. For each task, create ONE file in /handoff/pending/.\\n97|3. File name: [YYYY-MM-DD]_[SEQUENCE]_[AGENT-TARGET]_[TASK-SLUG].md\\n98|4. File content: use the exact template from CLAUDE_TO_HERMES_HANDOFF.md. No missing sections.\\n99|5. Be extremely specific: exact file paths, exact function names, exact Hebrew phrases where relevant.\\n100|6. Never touch /handoff/in-progress/, /handoff/completed/, /handoff/failed/.\\n101|7. After Hermes completes a task, review /handoff/completed/ and /handoff/failed/.\\n102|8. If a task failed, write a NEW clarified spec in /handoff/pending/. Do not edit the failed file.\\n103|\\n104|## Rules\\n105|- One task per file.\\n106|- If unsure about a detail, read the codebase first, then write the spec.\\n107|- Hebrew content inside specs must follow waocopy rules (singular male, 12-15 words per sentence).\\n108|- Never run deploy.sh. Eitan deploys manually.\\n109|\", \"total_lines\": 108, \"file_size\": 6463, \"truncated\": false, \"is_binary\": false, \"is_image\": false}", "tool_call_id": "call_873e1ebdb1d544c6920a1c77", "tool_calls": null, "tool_name": "read_file", "effect_disposition": null, "timestamp": 1787584697.0832658, "token_count": null, "finish_reason": null, "reasoning": null, "reasoning_content": null, "reasoning_details": null, "codex_reasoning_items": null, "codex_message_items": null, "platform_message_id": null, "observed": 0, "active": 1, "compacted": 0}, {"id": 266, "session_id": "20260824_221719_f9c1b5", "role": "assistant", "content": "I've confirmed the repo is clean and ready. Now I'll run a systematic audit to map API routes to test coverage and trace the operator pipeline wiring.", "tool_call_id": null, "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_c61f0887404045529604fa17", "call_id": "call_c61f0887404045529604fa17", "response_item_id": "fc_c61f0887404045529604fa17", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "todo", "arguments": "{\"todos\": \n[{\"content\": \"Map API routes vs test coverage (mechanical cross-reference)\", \"id\": \"1\", \"status\": \"in_progress\"}, {\"content\": \"Inspect operator-task endpoint wiring (who calls it, approval flow)\", \"id\": \"2\", \"status\": \"pending\"}, {\"content\": \"Ve